From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:49:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 061/105] fix(adaptive): stop the shared draft-extend backend
 being re-sized per state

--speculative-adaptive died with an illegal memory access on
Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (NEXTN, topk=1) at the first decode after warmup.

Root cause: for compressed-QSA draft models
DraftBackendFactory.create_draft_extend_backend() (draft_utils.py:139)
returns draft_model_runner.attn_backend rather than a fresh backend, so
every adaptive runtime state's EAGLEDraftExtendCudaGraphRunner shares one
backend object.  Its __init__ calls
draft_extend_attn_backend.init_cuda_graph_state(max_bs, max_num_token),
which in QwenSparseAttnBackend (qwen_sparse_attn_backend.py:897) *replaces*
_graph_seq_lens / _graph_compressed_page_table / _graph_fa2_* and friends.
Building the steps=3 and steps=7 states therefore freed exactly the tensors
the already-captured steps=15 draft-extend graphs point at.

- init_cuda_graph_state_no_shrink(): under adaptive, size the backend once
  (the launch state is the largest) and skip every later, smaller request;
  sharing the metadata tensors between states is safe because they are
  refilled immediately before each capture/replay.  Non-adaptive servers
  keep the plain upstream call.
- AdaptiveController.validate_candidates(): reject candidate_steps above
  --speculative-num-steps at start-up.  Every start-up-sized buffer (KV /
  mamba reservation, chain buffers, that graph state) is dimensioned for the
  launch value, so a larger candidate cannot be served.
- AdaptiveStepSlot: opt-in reset_ema_on_switch.  One EMA is shared by all
  candidates but its scale is bounded by the current step count, so an EMA
  that justified leaving steps=15 reads as a near-perfect chain at steps=3
  and bounces straight back.  Re-seeding at target-1 turns each switch into
  a probe the next update confirms or undoes.
- adaptive_w16_3_15.json: the tuned two-state config for this model
  (drop 15->3 at ema<=6.0, rise 3->15 at ema>2.35, EMA reseeded).

CPU tests cover the sizing invariants and simulate the shipped config
against the measured per-workload acceptance.

